# Vercel Speed Insights Installation Report

## Summary
Successfully installed and configured Vercel Speed Insights for the NDC Website Next.js project.

## Implementation Details

### 1. Documentation Retrieved
- Fetched the latest installation instructions from https://vercel.com/docs/speed-insights/quickstart
- Confirmed Next.js App Router (v13.5+) integration approach

### 2. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/speed-insights` version 2.0.0
- Package manager: npm (identified via package-lock.json)
- Command used: `npm install @vercel/speed-insights`

### 3. Code Changes

#### Modified Files:
1. **package.json**
   - Added `@vercel/speed-insights` to dependencies (version ^2.0.0)

2. **package-lock.json**
   - Updated with new dependency tree for @vercel/speed-insights and its dependencies (247 packages added)

3. **src/app/layout.tsx**
   - Added import: `import { SpeedInsights } from '@vercel/speed-insights/next';`
   - Added `<SpeedInsights />` component at the end of the body, right after the existing `<Analytics />` component
   - Follows Next.js App Router best practices for root layout integration

### 4. Integration Approach
- Used the Next.js-specific import path: `@vercel/speed-insights/next`
- Placed the SpeedInsights component alongside the existing Vercel Analytics component
- Preserved all existing code structure and functionality
- No breaking changes introduced

### 5. Testing & Verification
- ✅ Build completed successfully: `npm run build` passed
- ✅ Type checking passed (included in Next.js build)
- ✅ Generated 42 static pages without errors
- ✅ All existing routes compiled successfully
- Note: ESLint not configured in project (prompted for setup during lint command)

### 6. Technical Notes
- Project: Next.js 15.2.6 using App Router
- Framework: Next.js with TypeScript
- Styling: Tailwind CSS, Mantine UI
- Already had Vercel Analytics installed, Speed Insights complements it
- Speed Insights will work alongside existing Analytics implementation

### 7. Usage
Speed Insights is now active and will automatically:
- Track Core Web Vitals (LCP, FID, CLS, FCP, TTFB)
- Collect real user monitoring (RUM) data
- Send performance metrics to Vercel dashboard
- Provide insights on page performance

To view the data:
1. Deploy the application to Vercel
2. Navigate to Speed Insights in the Vercel dashboard
3. Enable Speed Insights for the project if not already enabled

## Files Changed
- `package.json` - Added @vercel/speed-insights dependency
- `package-lock.json` - Updated with new dependencies
- `src/app/layout.tsx` - Added SpeedInsights import and component

## No Breaking Changes
All changes are purely additive. The Speed Insights component:
- Does not affect existing functionality
- Loads asynchronously
- Has minimal performance impact
- Works seamlessly with existing Vercel Analytics
